<div><div class=3D"grid-cols-1 grid gap-2.5 [&amp;_>_*]:min-w-0"><h1 class=
=3D"text-2xl font-bold mt-1 text-text-100">NAB Senior Frontend Engineer Tec=
hnical Interview - Web Worker Data Transfer</h1>
<hr>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5"><strong>Part 1: =
Warm-up &amp; Introduction</strong></h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm David Chen, Principal Frontend Engineer here at NAB. I've b=
een with the bank for about 12 years now, working on everything from our di=
gital banking platforms to our internal trading systems. Today we'll be dis=
cussing some advanced frontend concepts, particularly around Web Workers an=
d large data handling - topics that are quite relevant to our work here giv=
en the scale of financial data we process daily.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Before we dive into the technica=
l deep dive, could you tell me a bit about your background and what drew yo=
u to apply for this Senior Frontend Engineer position at NAB?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Than=
ks David, great to meet you! I'm really excited about this opportunity. I h=
ave about 8 years of frontend engineering experience, with the last 4 years=
 focused heavily on React ecosystem and performance optimization at scale.<=
/p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">What drew me to NAB specifically=
 is the <strong>intersection of cutting-edge frontend technology with missi=
on-critical financial systems</strong>. In my current role at a fintech sta=
rtup, I've been leading the development of real-time trading dashboards tha=
t handle massive datasets - often processing 50-100MB of market data per mi=
nute through WebSockets. This experience has taught me that <strong>perform=
ance isn't just about user experience in banking - it's about accuracy, rel=
iability, and sometimes even regulatory compliance</strong>.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">I'm particularly interested in N=
AB's approach to <strong>micro-frontend architecture</strong> and how you h=
andle data synchronization across multiple trading desks and retail banking=
 interfaces. The scale and complexity here is something I'd love to contrib=
ute to and learn from.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's excellent background! Real-time trading dashboards are definitely simi=
lar to some of our challenges here. You mentioned handling 50-100MB of data=
 per minute - that's substantial. How did you approach the performance chal=
lenges there, especially regarding main thread blocking?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Grea=
t question! The main thread blocking was actually our biggest initial chall=
enge. When we first built the system, we naively tried to process all incom=
ing market data directly on the main thread - <strong>big mistake</strong>.=
 During high-volatility periods, we'd see UI freezes lasting 2-3 seconds, w=
hich in trading terms might as well be an eternity.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Our solution involved a <strong>=
three-pronged approach</strong>:</p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Web Workers for data pr=
ocessing</strong>: We moved all heavy computations - price calculations, te=
chnical indicators, risk metrics - into dedicated Web Workers</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Efficient data transfer=
 patterns</strong>: Instead of sending the entire dataset back and forth, w=
e implemented a <strong>diff-based update system</strong> using Transferabl=
e Objects</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Virtualization and prog=
ressive loading</strong>: Only rendering visible data points and using tech=
niques like react-window for the UI layer</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The most interesting part was fi=
guring out how to <strong>efficiently transfer large ArrayBuffers</strong> =
between workers without the performance penalty of structured cloning. We e=
nded up using a combination of SharedArrayBuffer for frequently accessed da=
ta and Transferable Objects for one-time large transfers.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: In=
teresting! You touched on Transferable Objects and SharedArrayBuffer - that=
's actually directly relevant to what we'll be discussing today. Before we =
dive deeper, I'm curious about your experience with the different state man=
agement solutions you mentioned in your resume - React Query, Redux, Jotai,=
 SWR, Zustand. In a high-throughput financial data scenario, which would yo=
u choose and why?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: That=
's a fantastic question because <strong>the choice really depends on the sp=
ecific data flow patterns and update frequencies</strong> we're dealing wit=
h.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">For high-throughput financial da=
ta, I'd actually recommend a <strong>hybrid approach</strong>:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>For real-time market dat=
a</strong> (high-frequency updates):</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Jotai or Zustand</stron=
g> - Their atomic update patterns and minimal re-render footprint are cruci=
al when you're getting price updates every 100ms</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Direct WebSocket =E2=86=
=92 Worker =E2=86=92 Atomic State</strong> pipeline bypassing traditional H=
TTP-based state managers</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>For user interactions an=
d CRUD operations</strong>:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>React Query or SWR</str=
ong> - Excellent for caching, background refetching, and handling server st=
ate</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Perfect for things like portfol=
io data, user preferences, account information</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>For complex application =
state</strong>:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Redux Toolkit</strong> =
- When you need predictable state transitions, middleware for logging/analy=
tics, and time-travel debugging for compliance purposes</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">In my previous role, we actually=
 used <strong>Jotai for real-time price atoms</strong> (each symbol had its=
 own atom), <strong>React Query for REST API data</strong>, and <strong>Zus=
tand for UI state management</strong>. This gave us the performance benefit=
s of atomic updates for high-frequency data while maintaining the DX benefi=
ts of React Query for traditional server state.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The key insight was recognizing =
that <strong>financial applications have fundamentally different data patte=
rns</strong> - some data changes 1000 times per second (prices), while othe=
r data changes once per day (account balances).</p>
<hr>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5"><strong>Part 2: =
Deep Technical Discussion - Web Worker Data Transfer</strong></h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent perspective on the hybrid approach! That segues perfectly into our =
main technical discussion. Let's talk about Web Workers and large data tran=
sfer.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Imagine we're building a new fea=
ture for NAB's trading platform that needs to process real-time market data=
 feeds - we're talking about CSV files containing 10-15MB of market data th=
at need to be parsed, analyzed, and visualized every few minutes. The data =
includes price histories, volume analysis, and risk calculations.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">How would you architect the data=
 flow from the initial file reception to the final UI update, specifically =
focusing on the Web Worker communication layer?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: This=
 is a really interesting architectural challenge that touches on several pe=
rformance-critical aspects. Let me break down my approach:</p>
